Differential tests for the top-level lib/ ports.

TWO KINDS OF ORACLE, and the second is better

  Transcribed  ref_lib.c holds C bodies copied out of the kernel by hand.
               Fine for a twenty-line function; a standing risk for anything
               larger, and a copy that drifts is worse than no oracle at all.

  Compiled     ref_sort.c is four lines: it #defines the exported names
               aside and #includes port/pristine/lib/sort.c ITSELF, built
               against the small shim headers in shim/linux/. Nothing is
               transcribed, so nothing can drift, and a change to the
               pristine file shows up as a test result rather than as
               silence.

The shims are deliberately minimal -- enough to compile one pristine file,
not a userspace kernel. Add to them only when a port needs it.
